Host sidecar regression tests
============================

After configuring the Windows build, run in WSL with Windows interop enabled:

  bash executor/tests/run-sidecar-tests.sh

The test compiles the actual fileDouble.c implementation into a Windows
executable, using CRT filesystem calls in place of Executor's host path
wrappers. It creates disposable fixtures under the workspace build directory.
No existing application files are modified.

Coverage: per-file and per-directory Netatalk, BasiliskII/SheepShaver and OS X
detection; compact 18-byte Finder records; raw resource forks at offset zero;
16-byte AppleDouble Finder records; unordered descriptors; truncated tables;
resource growth that preserves following metadata; and preserving the source
sidecar convention for a rename or move into a different-style directory;
moving/deleting companion pairs; and removing empty sidecar containers when
deleting a Macintosh directory.

The copy regression allocates a fresh AppleDouble resource fork, sets its EOF
again before writing, and reopens its descriptor. It checks the physical file
length, logical fork length and preserved seek position, then shrinks the fork.

For integration testing, launch a copy of a Macintosh application whose
metadata/resource forks are represented as .finf/name plus .rsrc/name, then
repeat with ._name AppleDouble. Confirm Finder type/creator, resource loading,
hidden sidecar entries, and no win_exception entries in stderr.txt.

Existing companions are preferred in this order: .AppleDouble/name,
.finf/name (or .rsrc/name), then ._name. New companions use the containing
directory's convention, detected from .AppleDouble, .finf/.rsrc, valid ._*
AppleDouble files, or .DS_Store. Unmarked directories retain Executor's
configured convention. Renames/moves preserve the individual file's format.
